// Formative practice quiz for conditional-display. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/components-and-state/conditional-display

::conditional-display o1 - one of two::A row shows either a Save button or a Saving message, never both and never neither. Which form fits best?{
~A single logical and expression#Not quite. That gives something or nothing, so one case would show neither.
~An if statement inside JSX braces#Not quite. Statements cannot go inside braces.
~Returning null from the component#Not quite. That renders nothing at all, not one of two.
=A ternary inside the JSX#Correct. A ternary selects exactly one of two outcomes.
}

::conditional-display o1 - whole screen early return::While a user object is missing, the entire page should be a sign-in prompt. What is the clearest code?{
~A ternary wrapped around every element in the page#Not quite. That works but repeats the condition or nests heavily.
~A logical and with the user object on every line#Not quite. That repeats the condition everywhere and still shows no prompt.
=An if before the return that returns the prompt early#Correct. An early return handles the whole-screen case and leaves the rest clean.
~Throwing an error when user is missing#Not quite. A missing user is an expected state, not an error.
}

::conditional-display o2 - stray zero::A component contains items.length followed by a logical and followed by a list. The list is empty. What appears on screen?{
=The number 0#Correct. The expression evaluates to 0, and React renders numbers.
~Nothing#Not quite. Nothing would appear for false or null, but this expression gives the number 0.
~The word false#Not quite. The value is 0, not false, and React never prints false.
~An empty list element#Not quite. The right side is never evaluated when the left side is falsy.
}

::conditional-display o2 - fixing the zero::How do you rewrite count followed by logical and followed by a badge so it can never show 0?{
=Compare first, so the left side is count greater than 0#Correct. A comparison yields true or false, and React renders neither.
~Wrap the badge in a fragment#Not quite. The stray value comes from the left side, so the fragment changes nothing.
~Add a key to the badge#Not quite. Keys identify list items and do not affect this expression.
~Use a string for count#Not quite. Numbers should remain numbers, and an empty string case would still need handling.
}

::conditional-display o3 - return null::A Warning component is told to show nothing. What does it return?{
~An empty div#Not quite. That still adds an element to the page.
=null#Correct. Returning null renders nothing.
~Nothing, by having no return statement#Not quite. A component that returns undefined is a mistake to rely on. Return null on purpose.
~The word null in quotes#Not quite. That would display the text null.
}
